  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 AM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nAs of 1 AM CST, temperatures across the region are in the mid 50s \nwith winds out of the southeast generally between 10 and 15 mph. \nThere are some areas with higher wind gusts, but this is mainly \naround the Sisseton Hills. \n\nA cold front and low pressure system will move across the area today \nbringing widespread rain. The heaviest rainfall will be through the \nrest of this morning. The low is expected to track right across the \nforecast area resulting in probable dry slotting, especially across \nsouthern portions of the CWA, which would reduce accumulations this \nafternoon. There is also a chance for some weak thunderstorms across \nsouth central and northeast SD this morning. Accumulations have a 40 \nto 70% chance of exceeding 2 inches between this morning and Tuesday \nmorning mainly north of Hwy 12 with highest chances west of US 281. \nAgain, most of this will fall this morning with lighter rainfall \nthrough the afternoon hours into Tuesday morning. Accumulations from \nnow through Tuesday evening have a 65 to 90% chance of exceeding 2 \ninches from the Missouri River east through west central MN. Chances \nfall off to the west of the Missouri. Looking past Wednesday, when \nwe could still see some light accumulations from wrap around \nmoisture, we see just some slight chances for light rain through the \nend of the period. \n\nAlong with the rain today, we have strong wind gusts of 45 to 55 mph \nacross central SD this afternoon. A Wind Advisory has been issued \nfor counties west of the Missouri River from 1 PM MST today through \n6 AM MST Tuesday for Corson and Dewey counties and 3 PM MST today \nthrough 11 AM MST Tuesday for Stanley, Jones, and Lyman counties (4 \nPM to noon CST). The REFS is showing a 50 to 70% of gusts of 45 mph \nor higher this evening into early Tuesday morning with the NBM \nshowing chances as 60 to 80%. These wind gusts are expected to \ncontinue through the overnight and into Tuesday morning across south \ncentral SD. Winds will die off a few hours after sunset Tuesday \nevening across the entire forecast area.\n\nTemperatures following this low pressure system will be about 10 \ndegrees cooler and right about average. This trend continues through \nthe end of the forecast period with some slightly above average \ntemperatures creeping right at the end of the period for central SD. \nAs mentioned above, we have some strong winds with this low pressure \nsystem that will continue through Tuesday. For areas east of the \nMissouri River, gusts will be in the 30-40 mph range. This is still \n6 days out, but models are starting to show some stronger wind gusts \nof 30-35 mph across central SD for Saturday.",
